' Inside one deploy evaluation: the single-order reduction scheduler of this
' branch against dev's racing evaluation (amendment of 2026-10-13). Both
' designs end in the same place: play writes a record, and replay follows the
' record. The left branch shows what the scheduler adds and which
' cost-accounting guarantees it supplies today. The right branch shows dev's
' evaluation and the four record-based designs RD-1 to RD-4 that a return to
' it would need. The proposal keeps the scheduler. It does not propose the
' return. It keeps the option possible.
'
' Code anchors (at e38ff0de8 unless named): deterministic_reduction.rs
'   split :92-133 · complete frontier :827-841 · one component :1068-1072 ·
'   key [2, 0] :1120-1122 · dev a06e3e7b3 reduce.rs:318-337 ·
'   dev ops_produce.rs:251-261 · dev docs/rholang-evaluation-order.md:5, 25-26.
' Source of truth: docs/casper/theory/cost-accounting-consensus-proposal.md §3.7.
' Palette: deploy input = supply blue · scheduler steps = process lilac ·
'   racing steps = control amber · RSpace candidate pick = gate teal ·
'   record = delta orange · replay = surface salmon ·
'   guarantees of the scheduler = green note · designs for a return = indigo note
' The legend also defines every name and abbreviation that the labels use,
' so the diagram reads on its own.
' Render:  ./render.sh consensus-proposal-scheduler-vs-racing.puml

@startuml consensus-proposal-scheduler-vs-racing
!include palette.iuml
$ca_base_skin()
skinparam ArrowColor $ca_neutral
skinparam ActivityBorderColor #333333
skinparam ActivityDiamondBackgroundColor $ca_control
skinparam ActivityDiamondBorderColor #333333
title Inside one deploy: the single-order scheduler (this branch) against racing evaluation (dev)

start
:a deploy body with parallel branches,\nfor example <latex>P \mid Q \mid R</latex>; <<$ca_supply>>
split
  :**This branch: the single-order scheduler**\nsplit the participant into child participants,\neach with a causal path (deterministic_reduction.rs:92-133); <<$ca_process>>
  :each child submits one RSpace intent and waits; <<$ca_process>>
  :complete frontier: every live participant\nwaits or has completed (:827-841); <<$ca_process>>
  :native execution adds the key [2, 0] to every footprint,\nso each native frontier is one conflict component\n(:1120-1122, assertion :1068-1072); <<$ca_process>>
  :run the intents one at a time, in causal order:\nthe single order of a native deploy; <<$ca_process>>
  note left $ca_note_green
    **What it supplies to cost accounting today**
    · equal replay host work at live map sizes
      (DR-78, DR-79 step 5, DR-100)
    · operation names by causal path
      (DR-74, DR-84 to DR-86)
    · order-dependent decisions without a record
      (the G3 design draft)
    · checkpoints that never see a partial evaluation
  end note
split again
  :**Dev: racing evaluation**\nspawn each branch as a Tokio task\nand await every handle (dev reduce.rs:318-337); <<$ca_control>>
  :the tasks race: per-channel locks serialize\none channel, and arrival order decides the rest; <<$ca_control>>
  note right $ca_note_indigo
    **What a return would need (RD-1 to RD-4)**
    · RD-1: replay admits operations in journal order
    · RD-2: names by RSpace source and occurrence
      (a protocol-6 evidence change)
    · RD-3: recorded G3 decisions, and settlement
      in recorded attempt order
    · RD-4: a replay stall verdict, which also fixes
      a latent hang of native replay at HEAD
  end note
end split
:RSpace picks one matching candidate:\nthe canonical order at e38ff0de8, and a random pick\nafter the restoration of dev's pick (dev ops_produce.rs:251-261); <<$ca_gate>>
:play writes the record: the event log\nand the native cost evidence; <<$ca_delta>>
:replay follows the record: the recorded COMMs,\nwhatever the order of the run; <<$ca_surface>>
note right $ca_note_grey
  Dev: "Rholang Is Non-Deterministic By Design"
  (docs/rholang-evaluation-order.md:5). Replay
  "uses the play event log to force the exact same
  COMMs during replay, regardless of evaluation order"
  (lines 25-26). Record and replay in §7 works
  under both branches of this diagram.
end note
stop

